## 2. Combination Sum I — Repeated Values Allowed

### Problem Statement

Given a list of **distinct** candidate numbers and a target `T`, find all **unique combinations** where chosen numbers sum to `T`. The **same number may be used multiple times**.

```
Input:  candidates = [2, 3, 6, 7],  target = 7
Output: [[2,2,3], [7]]
```

### Key Rules
- Each candidate can be picked **unlimited times**.
- The combination `[2,2,3]` and `[3,2,2]` are the **same** combination (order doesn't matter).
- No duplicate combinations in the result.

### How Repetition is Allowed

The trick: when you **pick** an element, you **stay at the same index** (allowing it to be picked again). When you **don't pick**, you **move to the next index**.

```
Pick element at index i  →  recurse with index = i   (same index, allows reuse)
Skip element at index i  →  recurse with index = i+1 (move on)
```

### Algorithm

```
combinationSum(candidates, index, target, current, result):
    BASE CASES:
        if target == 0:
            result.add(copy of current)   ← Found valid combination
            return
        if index == candidates.length OR target < 0:
            return                         ← Exhausted options or overshot

    // PICK: include candidates[index], stay at same index
    current.add(candidates[index])
    combinationSum(candidates, index, target - candidates[index], current, result)
    current.remove(last)                   ← BACKTRACK

    // NOT PICK: skip candidates[index], move to next
    combinationSum(candidates, index + 1, target, current, result)
```

### Recursion Tree — candidates=[2,3,6,7], target=7

```
f(i=0, T=7, [])
├── Pick 2  → f(i=0, T=5, [2])
│   ├── Pick 2  → f(i=0, T=3, [2,2])
│   │   ├── Pick 2  → f(i=0, T=1, [2,2,2])
│   │   │   ├── Pick 2  → f(i=0, T=-1, [2,2,2,2]) → OVERSHOT ✗
│   │   │   └── Skip 2  → f(i=1, T=1, [2,2,2])
│   │   │       ├── Pick 3  → f(i=1, T=-2) → OVERSHOT ✗
│   │   │       └── Skip 3  → f(i=2, T=1) → all remaining > 1 → ✗
│   │   └── Skip 2  → f(i=1, T=3, [2,2])
│   │       ├── Pick 3  → f(i=1, T=0, [2,2,3]) → TARGET HIT ✅ → ADD [2,2,3]
│   │       └── Skip 3  → f(i=2, T=3, [2,2])
│   │           ├── Pick 6  → OVERSHOT ✗
│   │           └── Skip 6  → f(i=3, T=3) → 7>3 → ✗
│   └── Skip 2  → f(i=1, T=5, [2])
│       ├── Pick 3  → f(i=1, T=2, [2,3])
│       │   └── (all paths lead to overshot or exhausted) ✗
│       └── Skip 3  → ... (6>5, 7>5) ✗
├── Skip 2  → f(i=1, T=7, [])
│   ├── Pick 3  → f(i=1, T=4, [3])
│   │   └── (no valid combination) ✗
│   ├── Skip 3  → f(i=2, T=7, [])
│   │   ├── Pick 6  → f(i=2, T=1, [6]) → overshot or exhausted ✗
│   │   └── Skip 6  → f(i=3, T=7, [])
│   │       ├── Pick 7  → f(i=3, T=0, [7]) → TARGET HIT ✅ → ADD [7]
│   │       └── Skip 7  → f(i=4) → exhausted ✗

RESULT: [[2,2,3], [7]]
```

### Pruning Optimization

Sort the candidates array first. If `candidates[i] > target`, all subsequent elements are also too large → **prune entire branch**.

```
if (candidates[index] > target) return;  // Sorted array → prune
```

### Java Code

```java
import java.util.*;

public class CombinationSumI {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum(int[] candidates, int target) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Arrays.sort(candidates); // Sort for pruning
        backtrack(candidates, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private void backtrack(int[] candidates, int index, int target,
                           List<Integer> current, List<List<Integer>> result) {
        // Base case: target reached
        if (target == 0) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
            return;
        }

        for (int i = index; i < candidates.length; i++) {
            // Pruning: sorted array, so all further elements also too large
            if (candidates[i] > target) break;

            current.add(candidates[i]);
            // i (not i+1) → allows reuse of same element
            backtrack(candidates, i, target - candidates[i], current, result);
            current.remove(current.size() - 1); // Backtrack
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CombinationSumI sol = new CombinationSumI();
        System.out.println(sol.combinationSum(new int[]{2,3,6,7}, 7));
        // Output: [[2,2,3],[7]]
        System.out.println(sol.combinationSum(new int[]{2,3,5}, 8));
        // Output: [[2,2,2,2],[2,3,3],[3,5]]
    }
}
```

### Time & Space Complexity

| | Complexity | Notes |
|--|--|--|
| **Time** | O(N^(T/M)) | N=candidates, T=target, M=min candidate |
| **Space** | O(T/M) | Max recursion depth = target/smallest |

