## 3. Combination Sum II — No Repetition (Unique Elements)

### Problem Statement

Given candidates that **may contain duplicates**, find all unique combinations that sum to `T`. Each number may be used **only once**.

```
Input:  candidates = [10, 1, 2, 7, 6, 1, 5],  target = 8
Output: [[1,1,6], [1,2,5], [1,7], [2,6]]
```

### Key Differences from Combination Sum I

| Aspect | Sum I | Sum II |
|--------|-------|--------|
| Input | Distinct values | May have duplicates |
| Reuse | Same element unlimited times | Each element used once |
| Index progression | Stay at `i` when picking | Always move to `i+1` |
| Duplicate handling | Not needed (distinct input) | Must skip duplicate siblings |

### The Duplicate Skipping Rule

After sorting, identical values appear consecutively. At the **same recursion level** (same position in the tree), if we've already explored starting with value `v`, we skip all subsequent `v`s — they'd produce identical subsets.

```
candidates = [1, 1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 10]  (sorted)

At level 0, index 0:
  Pick candidates[0]=1 → explore branch
  candidates[1]=1 → SAME VALUE at same level → SKIP
  Pick candidates[2]=2 → explore branch
  ... and so on
```

### Algorithm

```
combinationSum2(candidates, index, target, current, result):
    if target == 0:
        result.add(copy of current)
        return

    for i from index to end:
        // SKIP DUPLICATE: same value already tried at this level
        if i > index AND candidates[i] == candidates[i-1]:
            continue

        if candidates[i] > target: break  // Pruning

        current.add(candidates[i])
        combinationSum2(candidates, i+1, target-candidates[i], current, result)
        //                           ↑ i+1 (NOT i): each element used once
        current.remove(last)  // Backtrack
```

### Recursion Tree — candidates=[1,1,2,5,6,7,10], target=8

```
Sorted: [1, 1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 10]
         0  1  2  3  4  5   6

f(i=0, T=8, [])
├── Pick 1 (i=0)  → f(i=1, T=7, [1])
│   ├── Pick 1 (i=1)  → f(i=2, T=6, [1,1])
│   │   ├── Pick 2  → f(i=3, T=4, [1,1,2]) → no valid → ✗
│   │   ├── Pick 5  → f(i=4, T=1, [1,1,5]) → no valid → ✗
│   │   ├── Pick 6  → f(i=5, T=0, [1,1,6]) → ✅ ADD [1,1,6]
│   │   └── Pick 7  → OVERSHOT ✗
│   ├── Pick 2 (i=2)  → f(i=3, T=5, [1,2])
│   │   ├── Pick 5  → f(T=0, [1,2,5]) → ✅ ADD [1,2,5]
│   │   ├── Pick 6  → OVERSHOT ✗
│   │   └── ...
│   ├── Pick 5 (i=3)  → f(T=2, [1,5]) → no valid → ✗
│   ├── Pick 6 (i=4)  → f(T=1, [1,6]) → no valid → ✗
│   └── Pick 7 (i=5)  → f(T=0, [1,7]) → ✅ ADD [1,7]
│
├── SKIP 1 (i=1) → i>index AND candidates[1]==candidates[0] → SKIP ✗
│
├── Pick 2 (i=2)  → f(i=3, T=6, [2])
│   ├── Pick 5  → f(T=1, [2,5]) → ✗
│   ├── Pick 6  → f(T=0, [2,6]) → ✅ ADD [2,6]
│   └── ...
├── Pick 5 (i=3)  → no valid → ✗
├── Pick 6 (i=4)  → no valid → ✗
├── Pick 7 (i=5)  → no valid → ✗
└── Pick 10 (i=6) → OVERSHOT ✗

RESULT: [[1,1,6], [1,2,5], [1,7], [2,6]]
```

### Java Code

```java
import java.util.*;

public class CombinationSumII {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum2(int[] candidates, int target) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Arrays.sort(candidates); // CRITICAL: sort to group duplicates
        backtrack(candidates, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private void backtrack(int[] candidates, int index, int target,
                           List<Integer> current, List<List<Integer>> result) {
        if (target == 0) {
            result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
            return;
        }

        for (int i = index; i < candidates.length; i++) {
            // Skip duplicate values at the same recursion level
            if (i > index && candidates[i] == candidates[i - 1]) continue;

            // Pruning: sorted → no point continuing
            if (candidates[i] > target) break;

            current.add(candidates[i]);
            backtrack(candidates, i + 1, target - candidates[i], current, result);
            //                    ↑ i+1: each element used at most once
            current.remove(current.size() - 1); // Backtrack
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CombinationSumII sol = new CombinationSumII();
        System.out.println(sol.combinationSum2(new int[]{10,1,2,7,6,1,5}, 8));
        // Output: [[1,1,6],[1,2,5],[1,7],[2,6]]
        System.out.println(sol.combinationSum2(new int[]{2,5,2,1,2}, 5));
        // Output: [[1,2,2],[5]]
    }
}
```

