## 8. How Combinations vs Permutations Differ

```
COMBINATIONS: Order does NOT matter
  {1,2,3} = {3,2,1} = {2,1,3}   → counted as ONE
  Solution: Fix order by always moving index forward (i+1)

PERMUTATIONS: Order DOES matter
  [1,2,3] ≠ [3,2,1] ≠ [2,1,3]  → all THREE counted
  Solution: At each level, pick ANY unused element (from index 0)

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Decision: Is [A,B] the same as [B,A] for your problem? │
│    YES → Combination approach (index i+1 or stay)       │
│    NO  → Permutation approach (used[] from index 0)     │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Problem Classification Quick Guide

| Problem | Type | Repetition | Approach |
|---------|------|------------|----------|
| Combination Sum I | Combination | Allowed | Stay at `i` |
| Combination Sum II | Combination | Not allowed | Move to `i+1` + skip dups |
| Subsets | Combination | Not allowed | Stay/Move with not-take |
| Permutations I | Permutation | Not allowed | used[] from index 0 |
| Permutations II | Permutation | Duplicates in input | used[] + sort + skip |
| Phone keypad combos | Combination | Allowed | Multi-level recursion |

