### 🗣️ How to Explain in an Interview

**Step 1 — Classify (10 seconds)**
> "This is a combination/permutation problem. Order does/doesn't matter, and reuse is/isn't allowed."

**Step 2 — State the approach (20 seconds)**
> "I'll use backtracking. At each step, I pick an element, recurse, and then undo the pick. The base case is when the current path meets the target condition."

**Step 3 — Address duplicates if needed (10 seconds)**
> "Since there may be duplicates, I'll sort the array first and skip elements that are the same as the previous one at the same recursion level."

**Step 4 — Complexity (10 seconds)**
> "Time is O(2^N) or O(N!) depending on combination/permutation. Space is O(N) for the recursion stack."

### ⚡ Pattern Recognition Cheat Sheet

```
See "all combinations" → Backtrack with index advancing (i+1)
See "all permutations" → Backtrack with used[] from index 0
See "sum to target"    → Pass remaining = target - current
See "no duplicates"    → Sort + skip if nums[i]==nums[i-1]
See "reuse allowed"    → Stay at index i when picking
See "each used once"   → Advance to i+1 when picking
See "count only"       → Add memoization on top
```

### 💡 Template to Memorize

```java
void backtrack(input, index, current, result) {
    // 1. Base case
    if (goalMet) { result.add(new ArrayList<>(current)); return; }

    // 2. Explore choices
    for (int i = index; i < input.length; i++) {
        // 3. Skip duplicates (if needed)
        if (i > index && input[i] == input[i-1]) continue;

        // 4. Make choice
        current.add(input[i]);

        // 5. Recurse (i for reuse, i+1 for no reuse)
        backtrack(input, i or i+1, current, result);

        // 6. Undo choice (backtrack)
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);
    }
}
```

---

> 💡 **Key Insight**: Every backtracking problem is a tree traversal problem in disguise. Drawing the recursion tree before coding almost always reveals the structure you need.
